  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "This specimen gives partial evidence for session-layer composition because it cleanly separates outer-envelope dispatch from opaque payload semantics and makes unknown pCID failure explicit. But it does not model UDP-feed v0 itself, so it cannot show whether the UDP-feed API is sufficient for a next session layer or what binding details would leak.",
    "strengths": [
      "Clear dispatch boundary: pcid selects the payload handler and speculative payload parsing is forbidden.",
      "Deterministic CBOR plus mandatory signature slots make exact bytes and verification inputs locally recordable.",
      "Unknown-pCID store/forward with explicit unsupported-pCID failure fits sparse-knowledge, no-central-registry operation."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No UDP-feed API contract, session semantics, ordering model, or minimal group/session example.",
      "Draft status and unminted spec pCID weaken 100-year durability evidence.",
      "Relay or hop-local evidence may live in wrappers, payload, or signature slots, so next-layer placement is still somewhat underdefined."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "This could be over-read as proof of UDP-feed composition readiness when transport/session sufficiency was not tested.",
      "Mandatory outer signature slots may add coupling or overhead for minimal sessions.",
      "Opaque payload and signature handlers reduce generic auditability if future peers lack the referenced decoders."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What exact UDP-feed v0 operations does this envelope expect from the lower layer?",
      "How are retries, fragmentation, ordering, and peer-local session state represented above UDP-feed?",
      "What freeze and publication path will make future pCID-to-spec resolution durable for auditors?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
